{
  "analysis": "Final round. Evidence so far: (1) inclusion AP is flat at ~0.46 for 25-30 images and default scale (0.4678 @25 default, R2 C4) slightly beats small scale (0.4629 @30 small, R2 C2) — small pastes did NOT fix small-inclusion misses; (2) patches AP responds to allocation: 17→0.791 (R1 C4) vs 10-12→0.749-0.773 (R2), and patches are the most starved class (only 22 synthetic so far); (3) scratches need small scale (0.793 @10 small vs ~0.66 default) and 10 small-scale images already reached the best scratches AP, so extra scratches allocation is the uncertain lever. Design: three candidates that split the 50-image budget differently around the proven base (same_class, uniform sources, 0.15 overlap, scratches small-scale): C1 keeps the kept allocation but fixes inclusion scale (30/10/10); C2 shifts 5 images to patches (25/15/10) — my best bet; C3 shifts 5 to scratches (25/10/15) as the unsure alternative. All within headroom (inclusion ≤50, patches ≤83, scratches ≤82) and cumulative caps (max 85/37/38 ≤105).",
  "candidates": [
    {
      "hypothesis": "Inclusion scale at fixed allocation: R2 showed small scale (0.3-0.8) is neutral for inclusion (0.4629 @30) while default scale gave the best inclusion AP (0.4678 @25, R2 C4). Keeping the kept plan's allocation (30/10/10) but switching inclusion to default scale should hold inclusion AP50 >= 0.465 and mAP >= 0.670. Confirmed if inclusion >= 0.465 and mAP beats the control; if inclusion drops below 0.45, small scale was actually helping inclusion.",
      "allocation": {"inclusion": 30, "patches": 10, "scratches": 10},
      "params": {
        "inclusion": {"scale_min": 0.6, "scale_max": 1.4, "target_pool": "same_class", "source_weighting": "uniform", "max_overlap": 0.15, "sources": []},
        "patches": {"scale_min": 0.6, "scale_max": 1.4, "target_pool": "same_class", "source_weighting": "uniform", "max_overlap": 0.15, "sources": []},
        "scratches": {"scale_min": 0.3, "scale_max": 0.8, "target_pool": "same_class", "source_weighting": "uniform", "max_overlap": 0.15, "sources": []}
      }
    },
    {
      "hypothesis": "Allocation rebalance to patches: patches AP50 rises with allocation (17 -> 0.791 in R1 C4; 10-12 -> 0.749-0.773 in R2) while inclusion is flat between 25 and 30 (~0.46), and patches are the most starved class (22 synthetic so far). Shifting 5 images from inclusion to patches (25/15/10, inclusion default scale, scratches small scale) should lift patches AP50 to >= 0.77 and beat C1 on mAP. Confirmed if patches >= 0.77 and mAP is highest; if patches stay <= 0.76, the R1 0.791 was noise and inclusion should keep 30.",
      "allocation": {"inclusion": 25, "patches": 15, "scratches": 10},
      "params": {
        "inclusion": {"scale_min": 0.6, "scale_max": 1.4, "target_pool": "same_class", "source_weighting": "uniform", "max_overlap": 0.15, "sources": []},
        "patches": {"scale_min": 0.6, "scale_max": 1.4, "target_pool": "same_class", "source_weighting": "uniform", "max_overlap": 0.15, "sources": []},
        "scratches": {"scale_min": 0.3, "scale_max": 0.8, "target_pool": "same_class", "source_weighting": "uniform", "max_overlap": 0.15, "sources": []}
      }
    },
    {
      "hypothesis": "Scratches allocation at small scale: small scale is proven for scratches (0.793 @10 small vs ~0.66 default) but the allocation effect at small scale is untested (R1 C1: 16 small -> 0.746, confounded by other changes). Giving scratches 15 small-scale images (25/10/15) tests whether scratches AP50 can exceed 0.795 or whether 10 images was already the ceiling, and whether inclusion tolerates dropping to 25. Confirmed if scratches AP50 > 0.795 with mAP within 0.005 of the best; if scratches stay <= 0.79, extra small scratches are wasted budget.",
      "allocation": {"inclusion": 25, "patches": 10, "scratches": 15},
      "params": {
        "inclusion": {"scale_min": 0.6, "scale_max": 1.4, "target_pool": "same_class", "source_weighting": "uniform", "max_overlap": 0.15, "sources": []},
        "patches": {"scale_min": 0.6, "scale_max": 1.4, "target_pool": "same_class", "source_weighting": "uniform", "max_overlap": 0.15, "sources": []},
        "scratches": {"scale_min": 0.3, "scale_max": 0.8, "target_pool": "same_class", "source_weighting": "uniform", "max_overlap": 0.15, "sources": []}
      }
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "All four candidates fell below the pre-round mAP (0.6702), exposing that the R2-kept plan's 30 small-scale inclusion pastes were a liability that every R3 build inherits. The control (C4, repeating that plan) collapsed inclusion to 0.316, proving accumulated small inclusion pastes actively hurt. Default-scale inclusion beat small (C1 0.3813 > C4 0.316). C2 (25 default inclusion + 15 patches + 10 small scratches) won on BOTH mAP50 (0.6555, highest) and worst-class AP50 (0.5198, the round's best inclusion), so it clears the keep rule on mAP alone (next best C1 is 0.011 behind, >0.005). C3's extra small scratches (15) dropped scratches to 0.7547, confirming 10 small-scale scratches was the ceiling. C2's hypothesis was only half-confirmed (mAP highest, but patches 0.7271 < 0.77); the real win was inclusion jumping to 0.5198.",
  "choice": 2,
  "lesson": "Accumulated small-scale inclusion pastes are harmful: the C4 control (30+30 small) collapsed inclusion to 0.316, while default-scale inclusion is better (C1 0.3813) and C2's 25 default inclusion + 15 patches gave the best inclusion (0.5198) and mAP (0.6555). Scratches peaked at 10 small-scale images (0.793 in R2); adding more (15) hurt (0.7547). For any future run: cap small-scale inclusion, prefer default-scale inclusion, shift spare budget to patches, and do not over-allocate scratches."
}
